+Note that RO-Crate metadata is flattened, so a property value can either be a
+plain value (e.g., a string) or a reference to another entity, represented as a
+dictionary with an `@id`. For this reason, setting a property to a dictionary
+that does not have an `@id` raises a `ValueError`: add the referenced entity to
+the crate first (with `crate.add`) and assign that entity instead. Inline
+objects (dictionaries without an `@id`) that are found when reading an existing
+crate are still returned as they are by `__getitem__`.
+
 You can also add whole directories together with their contents. In an RO-Crate, a directory is represented by the `Dataset` entity. Create a directory with some placeholder files:
 
 ```bash
